WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:09.417
It was Christmas Eve, and the city of London was full of holiday cheer.

2
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:16.542
Despite the bitter cold, people were smiling and laughing as they went about their business.

3
00:00:16.543 --> 00:00:21.843
Even the horses pulling carts and wagons seemed jollier than usual.

4
00:00:21.844 --> 00:00:27.427
But not everyone was feeling the warm Christmas spirit.

5
00:00:27.428 --> 00:00:34.200
A man named Ebenezer Scrooge was still hard at work.

6
00:00:34.201 --> 00:00:40.973
The air inside his countinghouse office was nearly as cold as the air outside.

7
00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:45.131
Scrooge could afford to keep the building warm if he liked.

8
00:00:45.132 --> 00:00:50.660
But he believed in making and saving money, not spending it.

9
00:00:50.661 --> 00:00:58.019
He was so miserly that he allowed himself to light only a very small fire.

10
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:04.533
He allowed his employee, Bob Cratchit, an even smaller one.

11
00:01:04.534 --> 00:01:09.106
As the two men worked, the door suddenly flew open.

12
00:01:09.107 --> 00:01:12.584
A smiling young man burst in.

13
00:01:12.585 --> 00:01:19.105
"Merry Christmas, Uncle Scrooge!" It was Scrooge's nephew, Fred.

14
00:01:19.106 --> 00:01:24.838
"Bah!" Scrooge said. "Humbug!"

15
00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:28.152
Fred's eyes sparkled with amusement.

16
00:01:28.153 --> 00:01:31.098
"Are you calling Christmas a humbug, Uncle?

17
00:01:31.099 --> 00:01:37.932
I'm sure you don't mean to say that this wonderful holiday is a bunch of nonsense."

18
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:42.445
"I certainly do mean that," Scrooge said.

19
00:01:42.446 --> 00:01:48.142
"I hate Christmas! And why are you so merry?

20
00:01:48.143 --> 00:01:53.487
It's not as if you're wealthy, with nothing to worry about."

21
00:01:53.488 --> 00:01:57.539
"I won't disagree with that," Fred said cheerfully.

22
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.634
"I'm not a rich man. But why are you so miserable?

23
00:02:02.635 --> 00:02:08.070
Unlike me, you are wealthy. So shouldn't you be cheerful?"

24
00:02:08.071 --> 00:02:10.939
Scrooge rolled his eyes.

25
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:17.414
"I can't believe I live in a world full of fools like you."

26
00:02:17.415 --> 00:02:25.651
"Don't be so cross, Uncle." Fred grinned. "It's Christmas!"

27
00:02:25.652 --> 00:02:30.657
"You can celebrate Christmas in your own way," Scrooge said.

28
00:02:30.658 --> 00:02:34.755
"Let me celebrate it in mine."

29
00:02:34.756 --> 00:02:39.286
"Celebrate it?" Fred raised his eyebrows.

30
00:02:39.287 --> 00:02:42.299
"But you don't celebrate it."

31
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.103
"Let me ignore it then," Scrooge snapped.

32
00:02:46.104 --> 00:02:53.498
"In my opinion celebrating it has never done anyone any good at all."

33
00:02:53.499 --> 00:02:58.826
Fred shrugged. "Christmas has certainly never made me any money.

34
00:02:58.827 --> 00:03:03.879
But it is a kind, forgiving, and pleasant time.

35
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.309
It's a time when men and women open their hearts and try to help others.

36
00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.337
Therefore, Uncle, I believe it has done me good.

37
00:03:12.338 --> 00:03:17.177
And it will continue to do me good for as long as I live."

38
00:03:17.178 --> 00:03:22.989
Scrooge scowled. "You're a powerful speaker, Fred.

39
00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:28.233
You should run for a seat in Parliament."

40
00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:35.480
Fred laughed. "I haven't come to talk about money—or politics.

41
00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:39.581
I've come to invite you to dine with us tomorrow on Christmas Day."

42
00:03:39.582 --> 00:03:46.024
"Good afternoon," Scrooge said coldly, clearly dismissing his nephew.

43
00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:50.565
Fred tried once more to convince his uncle to come to dinner.

44
00:03:50.566 --> 00:03:57.123
But Scrooge's voice only got louder. "I said . . . good afternoon!"

45
00:03:57.124 --> 00:04:02.872
Finally Fred shrugged and gave up.

46
00:04:02.873 --> 00:04:06.479
"I can see that you're determined to stay angry.

47
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.537
Still, I wish you a merry Christmas, Uncle."

48
00:04:09.538 --> 00:04:13.108
"Good afternoon!"

49
00:04:13.109 --> 00:04:18.163
"And a happy New Year," Fred added with a grin.

50
00:04:18.164 --> 00:04:24.142
On his way out, Fred stopped to wish Bob Cratchit a happy holiday.

51
00:04:24.143 --> 00:04:31.999
Scrooge's eyes narrowed as he listened to the two men exchanging friendly words.

52
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:40.849
"Now Cratchit is the one talking about a merry Christmas for no good reason," Scrooge muttered to himself.

53
00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:44.729
"What right does that man have to be merry?

54
00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:51.410
The fool has chosen to try to support a wife and six children on a tiny salary.

55
00:04:51.411 --> 00:04:59.666
It's barely enough for one person, never mind an entire family."

56
00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:04.668
A few minutes later, Fred was gone.

57
00:05:04.669 --> 00:05:09.029
But two other people knocked on the door of the office.

58
00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:16.189
When Scrooge opened the door, he saw two older gentlemen standing there, smiling and well-dressed.

59
00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:23.098
"This business belongs to Scrooge and Marley, I believe?" one said in a pleasant voice.

60
00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:28.654
"Am I speaking to Mr. Scrooge or Mr. Marley?"

61
00:05:28.655 --> 00:05:34.229
"Marley has been dead for seven years," Scrooge said bluntly.

62
00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:42.275
He rarely thought about his old partner anymore, even though Jacob Marley had built the business along with him.

63
00:05:42.276 --> 00:05:47.655
"Oh. I'm sorry for your loss, Mr. Scrooge," the second man said with a bow.

64
00:05:47.656 --> 00:05:50.460
"We won't take up much of your time.

65
00:05:50.461 --> 00:05:55.453
We're only here to ask you to be generous during this festive time of year."

66
00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:58.977
"Yes," the first man added.

67
00:05:58.978 --> 00:06:02.280
"We're collecting donations for the poor.

68
00:06:02.281 --> 00:06:10.180
Many people lack even basic comforts of life, such as food and warm clothing."

69
00:06:10.181 --> 00:06:14.188
"That's why there are prisons!" Scrooge yelled.

70
00:06:14.189 --> 00:06:19.426
"I'd rather support those and let the poor be fed and warmed there.

71
00:06:19.427 --> 00:06:22.086
Good afternoon, gentlemen!"

72
00:06:22.087 --> 00:06:33.922
With that, he slammed the door in their startled faces and returned to work.

